    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_d82317f9",
      "@type": "OverallIntent",
      "instance_name": "overallintent_instance",
      "label": "the overallintent",
      "description": "meme-id: meme_image; hasOverallIntent: Harmful; overall-intent-description: The image depicts a historical scene of Nazi Germany with Adolf Hitler in a car, surrounded by Nazi supporters and crowds saluting. The overlaid text reads: 'it didn't happen because of the actions of one man' and 'it happened because of people who were 'just doing their jobs''. The overall intent is to shift blame for historical atrocities away from individual leaders and onto ordinary people who allegedly 'just followed orders' or 'did their jobs'. This is a harmful meme because it minimizes the moral responsibility of individuals, promotes a dangerous justification for complicity in genocide and totalitarianism, and can be used to downplay the role of leadership in enabling mass atrocities. It is not abusive or cyberbullying toward specific individuals or communities, but it is harmful as it normalizes and rationalizes participation in systemic evil, potentially encouraging passive acceptance of injustice or authoritarianism. The meme uses historical imagery to make an argument that is historically inaccurate and ethically dangerous, as it ignores the willful choices and moral failures of those who participated in the Holocaust and Nazi regime.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_7a6a1ff4"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
